---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const intCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'x^2', label: 'f(x) = x²', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: 'x^3/3', label: 'area-so-far A(x) = x³/3', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 3, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 9 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const kinCfg = {
  title: 'Worked example: from velocity to distance',
  steps: [
    { math: 'v(t) = 20 − 4t  (m/s)', note: 'a car braking evenly: 20 m/s slowing to rest.' },
    { math: 'displacement = ∫₀ᵗ v = [20t − 2t²] from 0 to 6', note: 'anti-differentiate: ∫20 dt = 20t, ∫−4t dt = −2t².' },
    { math: '= (120 − 72) − 0 = 48 m', note: 'the signed area under the velocity graph between t = 0 and t = 6 s.' },
    { math: 'v = 0 when t = 5 s', note: 'the car stops at 5 s. For t above 5 the (braking) model would push it backwards — velocities after rest are not part of the physical story.' },
    { math: 'total distance = |area| up to stopping = ½ × 20 × 5 = 50 m', note: 'when the velocity changes sign, distance is the sum of absolute areas, not the net signed area. 48 vs 50 m — the difference matters in crash reconstruction.' },
  ],
};

const intIdeaCloze = {
  "text": "Multiplying rate × time only works when the rate is constant; the instant it varies, you must add up the contributions of infinitely many %%0%% constant moments. That 'add up the infinitely many thin slices' operation is the %%1%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "briefly"
    ],
    [
      "integral",
      "integral ∫ f(x) dx",
      "the integral"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Each slice holds the rate still for a moment; the elongated S names the whole operation."
};

const intSliceEst = {
  "prompt": "Four right-edge strips under x² from 0 to 1 give ≈ 0.469; left edges give 0.219. In the limit of infinitely thin strips, both meet at what value?",
  "unit": "",
  "answer": 0.333,
  "factor": 1.15,
  "why": "Exactly ⅓ — that limiting value is the definite integral ∫₀¹ x² dx, and the two-sided squeeze is the proof that it exists for any continuous function.",
  "context": "right sum overcounts on a rising function, left sum undercounts"
};

const intSignedJudge = {
  "statement": "A business losing money for a quarter contributes a positive amount to the annual total when its profit-flow is integrated over the year.",
  "answer": false,
  "why": "A negative rate subtracts from the total — to get genuine geometric area you must integrate |f|, splitting the interval at every crossing of the axis."
};

const intPowerSort = {
  "prompt": "File each integral under the rule that solves it:",
  "buckets": [
    "power rule",
    "outside the power rule"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "∫ 6x² dx = 2x³ + C",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "∫ cos x dx = sin x + C",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "∫ eˣ dx = eˣ + C — its own anti-derivative",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "∫ (6x² + 4x − 5) dx = 2x³ + 2x² − 5x + C",
      "bucket": 0
    }
  ],
  "why": "Raise the power by one, divide by the new power — the two exceptions are worth memorising as counterparts of the derivative table."
};

const intConstSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims about the +C is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Derivatives destroy constants: x² + 3 and x² − 7 both differentiate to 2x.",
    "Reversing recovers only the shape, not the height.",
    "The family x³/3 + C shares one slope.",
    "In a definite integral the +C must be found from a boundary condition first."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "In a definite integral the constants cancel between the top and bottom limits — no +C is needed.",
  "why": "A boundary condition picks the one member of the family that fits reality, but the definite integral never needs it."
};

const intFtcCompare = {
  "prompt": "Evaluating ∫₁³ (2x + 3) dx — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "slicing",
    "text": "sum f(x)·dx over infinitely many strips — a limit of slice sums"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "Fundamental Theorem",
    "text": "find any anti-derivative F and compute F(b) − F(a) — the constant cancels"
  },
  "why": "The theorem converts a problem about infinite sums into a problem about reversing the rules you already know — and here both give 14."
};

const intPropMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each integral property with its statement:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "linearity",
      "right": "∫ (af + bg) = a∫f + b∫g"
    },
    {
      "left": "additivity",
      "right": "∫ₐᶜ = ∫ₐᵇ + ∫ᵦᶜ — accumulate in stages"
    },
    {
      "left": "reversal",
      "right": "swapping the limits flips the sign — walking backwards negates every slice"
    }
  ],
  "why": "All three come free from the slice picture — and ∫ₐᵃ = 0: no interval, no accumulation."
};

const intAreaEst = {
  "prompt": "Between y = x and y = x², meeting at x = 0 and x = 1, the area is ∫₀¹ (x − x²) dx. What is it?",
  "unit": "",
  "answer": 0.167,
  "factor": 1.15,
  "why": "½ − ⅓ = 1/6 — the same subtraction is how economists compute consumer surplus and pharmacokinetics compares drug concentrations.",
  "context": "always integrate upper-minus-lower"
};

const intSubstOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild the substitution for ∫ 2x·(x² + 1)³ dx — what comes first:",
  "items": [
    "Spot an inner function whose derivative also appears outside",
    "Set u = x² + 1 with du = 2x·dx sitting right there",
    "Substitute: ∫ u³ du = u⁴/4 + C",
    "Differentiate back to check — the chain rule restores the 2x"
  ],
  "why": "Roughly: 'match the inside, grab its derivative' — substitution is the chain rule run in reverse."
};

const intSimpsonPlace = {
  "prompt": "Simpson's rule error shrinks with the fourth power of the strip width. Halve the strips and the error improves by what factor?",
  "labels": [
    "2×",
    "4×",
    "16×",
    "64×"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 1,
    "max": 64
  },
  "answer": 16,
  "tolerance": 0.6,
  "why": "16× — for ∫₀¹ e^(−x²) dx, four parabola-width strips already give five-decimal accuracy against the true value 0.7468."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/integration">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Mathematics lesson whose one true prerequisite is the intermediate
    lesson <em>The Derivative</em>: integration is differentiation run backwards, so the power
    rule you used there returns here in reverse, and the chain and product rules return as
    substitution and integration by parts. The intermediate <em>Exponentials &amp; Logarithms</em>
    lesson supplies eˣ, whose habit of being its own anti-derivative the rule table leans on,
    and the foundational <em>Quadratics</em> lesson's habit of reading graphs — parabolas,
    signed regions, areas — is used from the first figure onward.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define the definite integral as a limit of slice sums, and say what each part of the notation ∫ f(x) dx names;</li>
    <li>state the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus in both directions, and use it to evaluate definite integrals exactly;</li>
    <li>recover anti-derivatives with the power rule, substitution and integration by parts — and check each by differentiating back;</li>
    <li>distinguish signed area (net change) from total area, and split an integral at every axis crossing;</li>
    <li>explain when the trapezium and Simpson's rules take over from symbols, and why Simpson's accuracy grows so fast.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The derivative lesson took a function apart: given position, find instantaneous velocity.
    Integration runs the film backwards — given a <em>rate</em>, recover the <em>total</em>.
    The general shape of the question is always the same: something varies continuously
    (speed, water inflow, force along a surface, demand for electricity across a day) and you
    want the accumulated amount (distance, volume, work, energy). Multiplying rate × time
    only works when the rate is constant; the instant it varies, you must add up the
    contributions of infinitely many <em>briefly constant</em> moments. That "add up the
    infinitely many thin slices" operation is the <strong>integral</strong>, written
    ∫ f(x) dx — an elongated S for "sum", with dx naming the slice width.
  </p>
  <Widget no="12" caption="fill the blanks — the accumulation operation" widget="cloze" config={intIdeaCloze} />
  <p>
    The miracle that makes calculus a single subject rather than two: slicing-and-adding, and
    anti-differentiating, are the <em>same operation</em>. The function A(x) that measures
    accumulated area under f up to x turns out to have derivative exactly f. Newton and
    Leibniz both saw this — the <strong>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</strong> — and it
    converts a problem about infinite sums into a problem about reversing the rules you
    already know.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="area under x² accumulates as x³/3 — the red curve is the running total of the blue one's area" widget="plotter" config={intCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> there are no dials here, just two curves — treat them as
    a claim to check, not a picture to admire. Run a fingertip along the red accumulation
    curve: wherever it climbs steeply, the blue parabola is tall at the same x; wherever the
    parabola sinks, the red curve flattens. Slope of the running total equals height of the
    rate — the Fundamental Theorem before it has a name. Check the endpoints too: at x = 3
    the red curve reaches 9, exactly the full area under the parabola out to 3.
  </p>

  <p>
    Watch the two curves in the figure. At x = 1 the area under the parabola is ⅓; at x = 2
    it is 8/3 ≈ 2.67; at x = 3 it is 9. The red curve plots that running total — and its
    slope at any point equals the height of the blue curve there. Slope-of-accumulation =
    rate: the derivative and the integral undo each other, and each is the explanation of
    the other.
  </p>

  <h2>Where integrals come from: slicing</h2>
  <p>
    To estimate the area under f(x) = x² from 0 to 1, chop the interval into four strips of
    width 0.25 and pretend f is constant on each. Using the <em>right</em> edge of each
    strip as its height gives rectangles of heights 0.0625, 0.25, 0.5625 and 1:
    total ≈ 0.25 × (0.0625 + 0.25 + 0.5625 + 1) = 0.469. Using <em>left</em> edges gives
    0.219. The true answer is squeezed between them — the right sum overcounts (rectangles
    poke above the curve on a rising function), the left sum undercounts. Halve the strip
    width and both estimates move toward each other; in the limit of infinitely thin strips
    they meet at exactly ⅓ ≈ 0.333. That limiting value <em>is</em> the definite integral
    ∫₀¹ x² dx, and the two-sided squeeze is the proof that it exists for any continuous
    function. It also explains the "dx": each rectangle contributes height × width, and the
    sum of f(x)·dx over all slices is the notation's literal meaning.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="call the squeeze target" widget="estimate" config={intSliceEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the integral is a limit of slice sums — a definition, not a
    trick — and it counts <em>signed</em> area, so negative rates subtract from the total.
    Next comes the computational shortcut: rather than summing slices by hand, reverse the
    differentiation rules you already own and let the Fundamental Theorem do the adding.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the integral is "the area under the curve", full stop. It
    is the <strong>signed</strong> area: parts of f below the axis count negative, because a
    negative rate subtracts from the total. A business losing money for a quarter has
    negative profit-flow; integrating it over the year reduces the annual total rather than
    adding to it. To get genuine geometric area you must integrate |f| — splitting the
    interval at every crossing of the axis — which is exactly the distinction the kinematics
    example below makes vivid.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="true or false — signed area in the ledger" widget="judge" config={intSignedJudge} />

  <h2>The anti-derivative family and the +C</h2>
  <p>
    Reversing the power rule d/dx xⁿ = n·xⁿ⁻¹ gives the master formula
    <strong>∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C</strong> (valid for any n except −1). Raise the power
    by one, divide by the new power. So ∫ 6x² dx = 2x³ + C, and ∫ (6x² + 4x − 5) dx =
    2x³ + 2x² − 5x + C — integration, like differentiation, respects sums and constant
    multiples. Two standard cases sit outside the power rule and are worth memorising as
    counterparts of the derivative table: ∫ cos x dx = sin x + C (and ∫ sin x dx = −cos x + C),
    and ∫ eˣ dx = eˣ + C — the exponential is its own anti-derivative, the defining quirk of
    e.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="file each integral under its route" widget="sort-buckets" config={intPowerSort} />
  <p>
    The <strong>+C</strong> is not decoration. Derivatives destroy constants (both x² + 3
    and x² − 7 differentiate to 2x), so reversing the process recovers only the shape, not
    the height. The whole family x³/3 + C shares one slope; a boundary condition — "the
    area-so-far starts at zero when x = 0", or "the car started at the lamp post" — picks
    the one member of the family that fits reality. In the differential-equations lesson
    this selection step gets a name (initial conditions) and becomes the heart of the
    subject.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the +C confusion" widget="spot-error" config={intConstSpot} />

<p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the +C in an antiderivative means the answer is not really known." The constant appears because derivatives erase constants: x², x² + 7 and x² − 3 all have derivative 2x, so the antiderivative is a family of parallel curves differing by a constant. Nothing is missing — and in a definite integral the constants cancel between the top and bottom limits, which is why the area is exact anyway.</p>

  <h2>Definite integrals: the Fundamental Theorem at work</h2>
  <p>
    Part 1 of the Fundamental Theorem (the one the figure shows): the accumulation function
    A(x) = ∫ₐˣ f(t) dt has A′(x) = f(x). Part 2 is the computational payoff: to accumulate f
    from a to b, find <em>any</em> anti-derivative F and subtract:
    <strong>∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a)</strong>. The constant cancels — which is why definite
    integrals need no +C. Worked example: ∫₁³ (2x + 3) dx = [x² + 3x] from 1 to 3 =
    (9 + 9) − (1 + 3) = 18 − 4 = 14. Geometrically: the region is a trapezium with parallel
    sides f(1) = 5 and f(3) = 9 and width 2, area ½(5 + 9) × 2 = 14. The formula and the
    trapezium agree — for straight-line graphs the slice arithmetic is elementary, and
    calculus earns its keep only when the graph curves.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="two ways to evaluate one integral — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={intFtcCompare} />
  <p>
    Three properties follow directly from "slices add up" and are used constantly:
    <strong>linearity</strong> — ∫ (af + bg) = a∫f + b∫g; <strong>additivity</strong> —
    ∫ₐᶜ = ∫ₐᵇ + ∫ᵦᶜ (accumulate in stages); and <strong>reversal</strong> — swapping the
    limits flips the sign, ∫ᵇᵃ = −∫ₐᵇ, because walking the interval backwards negates every
    slice. Also note ∫ₐᵃ = 0: no interval, no accumulation.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each property with its statement" widget="match" config={intPropMatch} />

<p><em>Common misconception:</em> "differentiation and integration are two unrelated skills that happen to share notation." The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus says they are inverse operations: integrating a rate recovers the total, differentiating the total recovers the rate. That symmetry is the theorem — the single most useful bridge in applied mathematics, and the reason one symbol (the integral) can mean both an accumulation and an antiderivative.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> Part 2 of the Fundamental Theorem made evaluation mechanical —
    find any anti-derivative, subtract at the limits — and linearity, additivity and reversal
    came free from the slice picture. Next we put the machinery to work measuring the gap
    between two curves, then hand it calculus's classic job: turning a velocity graph into
    distance travelled.
  </p>

  <h2>Areas between curves</h2>
  <p>
    When one quantity is always above another, the integral of the <em>difference</em> gives
    the area of the gap — the geometry of "how much better is A than B". Between y = x and
    y = x² (which meet at x = 0 and x = 1, with the line on top in between):
    area = ∫₀¹ (x − x²) dx = [x²/2 − x³/3]₀¹ = ½ − ⅓ = 1/6. The same subtraction is how
    economists compute consumer surplus (area between the demand curve and the price line),
    how pharmacokinetics compares drug concentrations, and how error is measured between a
    model curve and data. If the curves cross inside the interval, split the integral at
    each crossing and integrate the difference piecewise, always upper-minus-lower.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the gap between the curves" widget="estimate" config={intAreaEst} />

  <h2>Integration in motion: kinematics</h2>
  <p>
    The most-used application in physics: since velocity is the derivative of position,
    position-changes are integrals of velocity, and acceleration integrates to velocity.
    Concretely, a car braking from 20 m/s with steady deceleration has v(t) = 20 − 4t. Its
    displacement over the first six seconds and the subtlety of "distance travelled":
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a full worked example, step by step — signed area (displacement) versus total area (distance)" widget="equationStep" config={kinCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards run one braking car through the entire lesson
    in five moves. Cards 2 and 3 anti-differentiate and evaluate: 48 m of signed area.
    Card 4 is the sanity check the model demands — the velocity reaches zero at 5 seconds,
    and the braking law knows nothing about stopping. Card 5 totals absolute areas up to the
    stop: 50 m. The 2-metre gap between the two answers is the signed-versus-total
    distinction wearing a seatbelt, which is why crash reconstruction integrates speed
    rather than velocity.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same logic runs every direction: integrate a current I(t) to get charge delivered,
    integrate power P(t) to get energy used (your electricity meter is an integrator),
    integrate a flow rate to get total volume. Anywhere a meter reads a <em>rate</em>,
    integration produces the <em>bill</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Techniques: substitution and by parts</h2>
  <p>
    Most integrals are not naked power rules; the two great undo-moves are the reverse of
    the two differentiation rules you know. <strong>Substitution</strong> reverses the chain
    rule. Spot an inner function whose derivative also appears outside:
    ∫ 2x·(x² + 1)³ dx has u = x² + 1 with du = 2x·dx sitting right there. Substitute:
    ∫ u³ du = u⁴/4 + C = (x² + 1)⁴/4 + C. Differentiate back to check — the chain rule
    restores the 2x, confirming the answer. Roughly: "match the inside, grab its
    derivative."
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="rebuild the substitution" widget="order" config={intSubstOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>Integration by parts</strong> reverses the product rule: from (uv)′ = u′v + uv′
    comes ∫ u·v′ dx = u·v − ∫ v·u′ dx. It trades one integral for another — the art is
    choosing u so the new integral is easier. Example: ∫ x·eˣ dx. Let u = x (its derivative
    x′ = 1 simplifies things) and v′ = eˣ (whose integral is unchanged): result
    x·eˣ − ∫ eˣ dx = x·eˣ − eˣ + C. The classic payoff is ∫ ln x dx, which looks hopeless
    until parts with u = ln x, v′ = 1 gives x·ln x − x + C. There is no general algorithm
    for symbolic integration — it is pattern recognition, which is why the numerical
    fallback below matters.
  </p>

  <h2>When symbols fail: numerical integration</h2>
  <p>
    Many important integrands — e^(−x²) (the bell curve), measured data, functions defined
    only by a table — have no elementary anti-derivative at all. Slicing still works: the
    <strong>trapezium rule</strong> joins sample points with straight lines and sums the
    trapezia; <strong>Simpson's rule</strong> joins them with parabolas and is startlingly
    accurate (its error shrinks with the fourth power of the strip width: halve the strips,
    16× better). For ∫₀¹ e^(−x²) dx with four strips, the trapezium rule gives ≈ 0.7430
    and Simpson's rule ≈ 0.7469, against a true value of 0.7468 — five-decimal accuracy
    from four parabola-width strips.
    Every flight simulator integrating forces sixty times a second, and every climate model
    accumulating radiative flux, is running a numerical integrator, because the functions
    involved simply cannot be anti-differentiated in closed form.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="drop the marker — Simpson's error gain" widget="place" config={intSimpsonPlace} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: state the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus in both of its parts, in your own words.</li>
    <li>Application: the braking car's signed-area answer is 48 m but its absolute-area answer is 50 m. Explain where the 2-metre gap comes from, and say which number a crash investigator should report.</li>
    <li>Why does a definite integral need no +C, even though every anti-derivative family carries one?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Part 1: the accumulation function's derivative is the
    original rate — the slope of area-so-far equals the height of the curve. Part 2: to
    accumulate from a to b, take any anti-derivative F and compute F(b) − F(a); together the
    parts say differentiating and integrating undo each other. (2) The braking law pushes the
    car backwards after its 5-second stop, and signed area dutifully counts that spurious
    reverse motion as a deduction: 50 − 2 = 48 m. An investigator wants metres of travel, so
    report total distance — absolute area, 50 m. (3) Derivatives erase constants, so an
    anti-derivative is a family of parallel curves; the constant appears in both F(b) and
    F(a) and cancels in the subtraction, leaving the exact accumulation. Nothing is missing —
    it never survives to the answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The integral is the limit of adding infinitely many briefly-constant slices — a definition, not a trick.</li>
    <li>Signed area is the default: negative rates subtract; genuine geometric area needs |f|.</li>
    <li>The Fundamental Theorem: the accumulation's derivative is the rate, and any anti-derivative F evaluates ∫ₐᵇ f as F(b) − F(a).</li>
    <li>The +C marks a family of parallel curves; a boundary condition picks the member that fits reality.</li>
    <li>Substitution reverses the chain rule; integration by parts reverses the product rule; check every answer by differentiating back.</li>
    <li>When no elementary anti-derivative exists — e^(−x²), measured data — trapezium and Simpson's rules slice numerically instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lesson <em>Differential Equations</em> picks up exactly where the +C
    discussion ended: initial conditions selecting one curve from the anti-derivative family
    become the heart of that subject. The advanced lesson <em>Vectors and Matrices</em> takes
    the linearity you have been using — integrals respecting sums and constant multiples —
    into a setting where whole systems of equations are transformed at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why the FTC is true (sketch).</strong> Let A(x) be area-so-far under a
    continuous f. Then A(x + h) − A(x) is the area of a thin slab; since f barely changes
    across a slab of width h, that area is squeezed between f(x)·h and f(x + h)·h (for
    increasing f). Divide by h and let h → 0: both bounds tend to f(x), so
    A′(x) = f(x). The whole theorem is the observation that a thin slab is almost a
    rectangle, plus a squeeze. The deeper requirement — that A exists at all for every
    continuous f — took mathematicians until Riemann (and later Lebesgue) to make fully
    precise, and the Lebesgue refinement is what modern probability theory is built on:
    a random variable's distribution is integrated to give probabilities, and E[X] =
    ∫ x·f(x) dx is literally an expectation integral.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Improper integrals: when infinite slices converge.</strong> Extend to infinite
    intervals by a limit: ∫₁^∞ f = lim as b → ∞ of ∫₁ᵇ f. The surprise is that some infinite
    regions have finite area: ∫₁^∞ 1/x² dx = [−1/x]₁^∞ = 0 − (−1) = 1. Yet ∫₁^∞ 1/x dx =
    [ln x]₁^∞ = ∞. Two curves that both approach zero, one of whose areas is finite and the
    other infinite — the boundary sits exactly at 1/x¹ (the "p-test": converges for powers
    p above 1, diverges for p at most 1). Rotate the finite one about its axis and you get
    <strong>Gabriel's Horn</strong>: a shape with finite volume but infinite surface area —
    you could fill it with paint but never paint it. Paradoxes like this forced the careful
    definitions of limit and convergence in the expert-tier analysis lesson.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond areas: the integral as an operator.</strong> Integration is linear,
    smoothing (integrating twice makes a kinked curve genuinely curved), and it inverts
    differentiation only up to the constant — the kernel of that failure, the constant
    function, is exactly the null space of the derivative. In higher mathematics the same
    slicing idea generalises to line integrals (work done along a curved path), double and
    triple integrals (mass of a plate with varying density, centre of mass, moments of
    inertia), and convolution integrals (how a signal is smeared by a system — the
    mathematics of echo, blur and every filter in audio processing). Fourier analysis, the
    engine of the spectroscopy lesson in chemistry, is the statement that a huge class of
    functions can be built from integrals of sines and cosines of all frequencies.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open frontier.</strong> Which functions have elementary anti-derivatives is
    fully settled (Liouville's theorem: e^(−x²), 1/ln x, and sin(x)/x famously do not), but
    efficient <em>numerical</em> integration in high dimensions is not: Monte Carlo methods
    — averaging random samples instead of summing grids — are the workhorse for the
    thousand-dimensional integrals of financial risk and particle physics, and improving
    their convergence rates remains active research. The humble idea of "add up thin
    slices" scales from homework to the LHC unchanged; only the notion of <em>slice</em>
    has to get cleverer.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, <em>Calculus Volume 1</em> (ch. 5, Integration); Stewart, <em>Calculus</em>; Courant &amp; John, <em>Introduction to Calculus and Analysis</em>.</p>
